Now I understand the latch. Now, let me examine the critical interplay question: the fail_validation rule records the 🔴 marker on each red wave. Let's look at the fail_validation local action code around line 3339 to see how the prior red count is read and marker written, and importantly to see the flow for the machine PR — does fail_validation itself re-arm the rework (via requeue agent:rework on the LINKED ISSUE)? And what happens after rework leg pushes and validate-fresh re-dispatches validation?

Actually, the key scenario for double-counting: on a machine PR with ai_validating label, fail-validation handles the red: reports + records 🔴 marker (red N/cap) + requeues agent:rework on the linked ISSUE. Then rework-on-red-ci (issue carrier) dispatches. When the rework leg pushes a fix, new head; validation re-runs. If red again on new head, fail-validation records red 1/2... on the new head — separate budget.

But wait: what about the case where fail_validation has recorded marker "red 1/3" on head H for an ARMED validation (ai_validating), then... the label ai_validating is removed (unarm), the issue carrier gets agent:rework... rework pushes nothing (empty lap), head still H... Now the PR has validation_failed? Let me trace: after fail-validation, the PR label ai_validating is removed and validation_failed added? Per rule descriptions: "GitHub PR: validation CI red on the head -> unarm ai_validating, report (with the 🔴 red-head counter marker — the validate_pr arm side skips heads with >= redHeadCap recorded reds), requeue agent:rework on the linked issue."

So fail_validation already handles the armed case end-to-end (marker + issue rework). The new red-gate rule covers reds where ai_validating was never stamped.

